{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T231601956950+1000.conduct_an_analysis_to_determine.output.json", "system": "You are the independent coverage verifier between a cited research draft and\nfinal acceptance. Compare the draft with the original request and the numbered\npre-research plan. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, judge citation\nsupport, or invent facts. You may check whether a citation marker is present;\ndo not decide whether the cited source actually entails the sentence.\n\nA requirement counts as covered only when the draft states it concretely. A\nrelated topic, generic synonym, or plan intention is not enough. Prioritize:\n- penalty prevention before optional coverage: flag any uncited sentence that\n  generalizes a health, safety, medical, legal, or causal outcome across a\n  population, even when it is a synthesis or takeaway sentence;\n- every explicit requested component and required deliverable form;\n- an element the user requires in every repeated post, example, or section;\n- named safety, legal, temporal, or jurisdictional constraints;\n- concrete prior studies, measurements, comparisons, or examples the plan\n  marked necessary to establish context;\n- definitions needed by the stated audience.\n\nA sentence saying that no exact study was established does not cover a plan\nitem requiring prior measured context. When the draft contains any related\nmeasurement, require it to name the closest credible precedent and state the\npopulation, outcome, or scope mismatch rather than leaving the context empty.\n\nRank gaps by expected coverage gained per answer word and by whether the live\nresearch context can repair them with evidence it likely already owns. Prefer\nmissing per-part structure, definitions, comparisons, and decisive evidence\nover replacing already-valid examples, diversifying sources for its own sake,\nor chasing a missing archive code. Do not require changing selected cases or\nfinding new provenance unless the original request explicitly makes that\nparticular identity or mix mandatory.\n\nPreserve the requirement's exact evidence type; never weaken it into an\nalternative. A model-performance metric is not a prevalence rate, incidence is\nnot prevalence, a date range is not a measured result, a generic example is not\na named case study, and covering an element in one post does not cover a demand\nthat it appear in every post. When a quantitative result is required, specify\nthat its value, population or sample, and time/scope are missing.\n\nDo not demand optional breadth or every suggestion in an otherwise complete\nplan. Return JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"plan_item\":10,\"requirement\":\"short concrete requirement\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or only generic\"}]}\n\nList at most four material gaps, ordered by importance. Use a null plan_item\nonly for an explicit user requirement absent from the plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.\n\nPRE-RESEARCH COVERAGE PLAN\n\n1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a domain-structured comparative analysis ending, after all domains, with an unambiguous scoped verdict—more beneficial or more detrimental—and identify the greatest-weight domain, explaining why it outweighs the others.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for educated general readers; define essential social-science, platform, and causal-inference concepts in accessible prose without sacrificing evidentiary precision.\n\n3. CORE: Define “social media,” “beneficial,” and “detrimental”; state period, geography, affected populations, and counterfactual baseline; judge effect magnitude, reach, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution, and causal confidence.\n\n4. CORE: Analyze information, democracy, and public safety: news access, participation, collective action, and accountability versus misinformation, polarization, extremism, harassment, and crisis harms.\n\n5. CORE: Analyze individual and relational well-being: connection, identity formation, support communities, and creative expression versus anxiety, depression, loneliness, compulsive use, body-image pressure, and youth-specific risks.\n\n6. CORE: Analyze economic opportunity, institutions, and rights: entrepreneurship, employment, education, health communication, and knowledge sharing versus surveillance, privacy loss, scams, labor exploitation, concentration, and unequal access or harm.\n\n7. CORE: Within every domain, contrast short- and long-run effects, winners and losers, platform design and user behavior, and disaggregate by age, socioeconomic position, and vulnerability.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: For every domain, separately label and support beneficial and detrimental effects using at least two academic studies, two journalistic reports, and two named platform case studies spanning at least two geographic regions.\n\n9. EVIDENCE: Distinguish correlational from causal evidence; include at least one randomized controlled trial and one natural experiment estimating social media’s effects, while addressing self-selection, reverse causality, null results, and methodological limits.\n\n10. LATENT: Explain how engagement-based recommender systems and platform incentives amplify or suppress individual behaviors into society-scale benefits and harms.\n\n11. LATENT: Use Myanmar as a named non-Western case showing benefits and harms within the same political or social setting, verified against credible reporting and primary documents where possible.\n\n12. LATENT: Compare the Digital Services Act with Section 230 through one concrete platform-liability or content-regulation example, showing how governance conditions platform incentives and the net judgment.\n\n13. SAFETY: Scope conclusions to the available geography and platform era; avoid universalizing Western evidence, and verify current statistics, laws, regulatory actions, ownership, and evolving research consensus.\n\n14. BUDGET: Allocate 80 first-draft words to framing, 60 to method, 180 per domain, and 160 to synthesis, totaling 840 words and reserving 184 words for evidence-grounded completion patches.\n\nCITED DRAFT TO VERIFY\n\nSocial media means platforms that let users create, distribute and algorithmically discover content and relationships; this assessment covers the mass-platform era from roughly 2004 to 6 August 2026, across the regions represented in the evidence, and compares it with a society retaining earlier websites, messaging, broadcasting and offline networks but lacking engagement-ranked mass feeds. \n“Beneficial” means a durable, broadly distributed improvement in welfare, knowledge, agency, opportunity or rights, while “detrimental” means the reverse; the balance considers magnitude, population reach, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution and causal confidence rather than merely counting effects. \nCausal confidence is strongest when random assignment or a natural experiment approximates the missing counterfactual, whereas surveys and cross-sectional associations remain vulnerable to self-selection and reverse causality, including the possibility that distressed people select more social media or polarized societies generate polarized feeds. [shard_03588_70930] [shard_01051_58704] [shard_03406_3576]\nIn information, democracy and public safety, the beneficial side includes rapid news access, political expression, participation, collective action, citizen documentation and emergency awareness: a 499-article academic review found the clearest gain in participation, while case studies record ordinary people exposing Abu Ghraib abuse, Iranian election violence and disasters, Myanmar journalists connecting citizens with officials and NGOs, and Facebook and Twitter supporting crisis communication. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01867_61565] [shard_02059_76178]\nThe detrimental side is larger and more durable because misinformation, harassment, institutional distrust, homophily and extremism scale cheaply, with academic work associating social media with detrimental changes in trust and all five negative indicators—hate, polarization, populism, network homophily and misinformation—and reports that, beginning in 2013, Myanmar military members were believed to have used fake Facebook identities for incendiary anti-Rohingya posts where Facebook was many people’s main or only route to online news and internet use. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01568_51801] [shard_00992_13016]\nMyanmar also exposes unequal winners and losers: in a survey of 1,340 young people concerning first-time voters’ political-news consumption, 80% reported social-movement involvement, yet Facebook had only two Burmese-speaking harmful-content reviewers for roughly 50 million people in early 2015, and Lu Nge Khit later reported that posts about conflict, torture, arson and killings were often removed. [shard_01247_18777] [shard_00992_13016] [shard_00569_38420]\nThe evidence nevertheless counsels against monocausal claims: Russian troll material was estimated at only 0.004% of U.S. Facebook-feed content, and studies of Facebook’s and Instagram’s recommendation algorithms around the 2020 U.S. election found increased engagement and time online but no detectable change in existing political attitudes or polarization, even as the algorithms increased exposure to uncivil content and reduced political content from moderate friends and ideologically mixed audiences. [shard_06261_13612] [shard_03177_74042]\nIn individual and relational well-being, benefits are genuine for geographically separated families, shy or marginalized young people, identity exploration, creative communities and pandemic-era support, while body-positive and recovery networks can improve mood and body appreciation; these gains concentrate among people whose offline alternatives are weakest. [shard_00741_18887] [shard_01581_66087] [shard_02027_72940]\nHarms include compulsive attention, comparison, anxiety, depression, loneliness, displaced face-to-face intimacy and body pressure, with a 50-study review across 17 countries identifying girls and people already concerned about weight as particularly vulnerable and a cross-sectional pilot survey of 700 northeastern U.S. middle-school students—52% of them from racial or ethnic minorities—finding social-media-related body dissatisfaction in 19% of respondents. [shard_03023_52901] [shard_01350_54628] [shard_00741_18887]\nCausal studies strengthen but circumscribe that conclusion: a three-week randomized limit of no more than 30 minutes total per day across Facebook, Instagram and Snapchat reduced depression and loneliness among 143 Penn students but produced null effects on support, self-esteem and overall well-being, while Facebook’s staggered introduction across 775 colleges—a natural experiment—was associated with 7% more severe functional depression and 20% more anxiety disorders, especially among socially isolated or indebted students. [shard_03588_70930] [shard_01051_58704]\nThus active, purposeful use can produce immediate and reversible connection, whereas passive comparison and recommender-driven repetition can accumulate into longer-lived harms, with adolescents, poorer users and people already depressed, isolated or body-conscious facing the least favorable balance and the weakest ability to opt out. \nIn economic opportunity, institutions and rights, benefits include inexpensive marketing, crowdfunding, professional networking, hiring, entrepreneurship, education and health communication: studies describe global knowledge access and collaboration, Australian small-business adoption, and MENA surveys in which 86% viewed social media as important to start-ups and 71% expected to use it for their next job. [shard_03385_45918] [shard_04212_39856] [shard_06366_43784]\nNamed health cases show Facebook subsidizing trusted vaccine campaigns, WhatsApp and chatbots connecting authorities with publics, Japanese platforms supporting COVID fact-checking, and Twitter patients coining and spreading “long COVID,” although a 31-study WHO review also found that medical misinformation increased fear, misunderstanding and barriers to care. [shard_01618_10932] [shard_01949_73119]\nAgainst those gains stand surveillance, scams, concentrated gatekeeping and hidden labor: Cambridge Analytica harvested millions of Facebook profiles without consent, U.S. consumers reported $770 million in social-media-originated fraud losses in 2021, and Kenyan Facebook contractors alleged PTSD after inadequately supported exposure to graphic moderation work. [shard_01677_30821] [shard_03319_35805] [shard_02380_17754]\nPlatform dependence also redistributes power from creators and institutions to opaque ranking systems, as seven Indian journalism start-ups found that engagement economics discouraged minority coverage and TikTok was reported to disadvantage minority, disabled and overweight creators, turning nominally equal access into unequal visibility and income. [shard_06536_4631] [shard_03462_20779]\nThe mechanism connecting all three domains is incentive design: ranking individual posts by engagement can give uniformly high scores to controversial material, while recommender-system manipulation can shape users’ views without their knowledge and extensive data collection creates additional security risks. [shard_03914_79668] [shard_03147_70901]\nGovernance can change that balance: Section 230 broadly protects U.S. online platforms from liability for user-generated content, generating partisan disputes over both viewpoint censorship and inadequate action against misinformation, whereas the European Union has opened an investigation into TikTok’s compliance with the Digital Services Act. [shard_00913_73926] [shard_06113_43175] [shard_00804_29144]\nOverall, social media’s impact in the engagement-optimized mass-platform era has been more detrimental than beneficial: its connection, knowledge and opportunity gains are substantial but often substitutable, targetable and reversible, whereas violence, youth developmental injury, privacy loss and institutional distrust are more unevenly imposed, persistent and sometimes irreversible. \nInformation, democracy and public safety receives the greatest weight because it has the broadest spillovers—corrupted information weakens health, markets, relationships and regulation simultaneously—and Myanmar demonstrates that platform failure can convert communication advantages into physical persecution, making this domain’s severe downside outweigh the other domains’ net benefits. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_00992_13016] [shard_05518_79195]", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"plan_item\":13,\"requirement\":\"Attach citations to population-level health, safety, and causal conclusions.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The uncited well-being synthesis beginning “Thus active, purposeful use...” generalizes causal mental-health effects and vulnerable populations. The uncited overall verdict also asserts violence and youth developmental injury as persistent or irreversible outcomes.\"},{\"plan_item\":8,\"requirement\":\"In every domain, provide at least two academic studies, two journalistic reports, and two named platform case studies spanning two regions.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The individual and relational well-being domain does not concretely identify two journalistic reports or two named platform case studies across two regions. The information/democracy domain also does not clearly identify two named platform case studies spanning two regions.\"},{\"plan_item\":7,\"requirement\":\"Within every domain, contrast short- and long-run effects, winners and losers, platform design and user behavior, and age, socioeconomic, and vulnerability differences.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The information domain lacks a socioeconomic disaggregation and a concrete short-versus-long-run comparison. The economic/institutions/rights domain lacks age and socioeconomic disaggregation and a short-versus-long-run comparison.\"},{\"plan_item\":12,\"requirement\":\"Compare the Digital Services Act and Section 230 through one concrete content-regulation or platform-liability example and explain the resulting incentive and net-impact difference.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The draft juxtaposes a general description of Section 230 with an EU TikTok investigation, but does not apply both regimes to one concrete example or show how their differing rules change platform incentives and the net judgment.\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"plan_item": 13, "requirement": "Attach citations to population-level health, safety, and causal conclusions.", "draft_gap": "The uncited well-being synthesis beginning “Thus active, purposeful use...” generalizes causal mental-health effects and vulnerable populations. The uncited overall verdict also asserts violence and youth developmental injury as persistent or irreversible outcomes."}, {"plan_item": 8, "requirement": "In every domain, provide at least two academic studies, two journalistic reports, and two named platform case studies spanning two regions.", "draft_gap": "The individual and relational well-being domain does not concretely identify two journalistic reports or two named platform case studies across two regions. The information/democracy domain also does not clearly identify two named platform case studies spanning two regions."}, {"plan_item": 7, "requirement": "Within every domain, contrast short- and long-run effects, winners and losers, platform design and user behavior, and age, socioeconomic, and vulnerability differences.", "draft_gap": "The information domain lacks a socioeconomic disaggregation and a concrete short-versus-long-run comparison. The economic/institutions/rights domain lacks age and socioeconomic disaggregation and a short-versus-long-run comparison."}, {"plan_item": 12, "requirement": "Compare the Digital Services Act and Section 230 through one concrete content-regulation or platform-liability example and explain the resulting incentive and net-impact difference.", "draft_gap": "The draft juxtaposes a general description of Section 230 with an EU TikTok investigation, but does not apply both regimes to one concrete example or show how their differing rules change platform incentives and the net judgment."}]}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2934, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1887}}
{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T231601950433+1000.i_m_currently_working_on.output.json", "system": "You are the independent coverage verifier between a cited research draft and\nfinal acceptance. Compare the draft with the original request and the numbered\npre-research plan. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, judge citation\nsupport, or invent facts. You may check whether a citation marker is present;\ndo not decide whether the cited source actually entails the sentence.\n\nA requirement counts as covered only when the draft states it concretely. A\nrelated topic, generic synonym, or plan intention is not enough. Prioritize:\n- penalty prevention before optional coverage: flag any uncited sentence that\n  generalizes a health, safety, medical, legal, or causal outcome across a\n  population, even when it is a synthesis or takeaway sentence;\n- every explicit requested component and required deliverable form;\n- an element the user requires in every repeated post, example, or section;\n- named safety, legal, temporal, or jurisdictional constraints;\n- concrete prior studies, measurements, comparisons, or examples the plan\n  marked necessary to establish context;\n- definitions needed by the stated audience.\n\nA sentence saying that no exact study was established does not cover a plan\nitem requiring prior measured context. When the draft contains any related\nmeasurement, require it to name the closest credible precedent and state the\npopulation, outcome, or scope mismatch rather than leaving the context empty.\n\nRank gaps by expected coverage gained per answer word and by whether the live\nresearch context can repair them with evidence it likely already owns. Prefer\nmissing per-part structure, definitions, comparisons, and decisive evidence\nover replacing already-valid examples, diversifying sources for its own sake,\nor chasing a missing archive code. Do not require changing selected cases or\nfinding new provenance unless the original request explicitly makes that\nparticular identity or mix mandatory.\n\nPreserve the requirement's exact evidence type; never weaken it into an\nalternative. A model-performance metric is not a prevalence rate, incidence is\nnot prevalence, a date range is not a measured result, a generic example is not\na named case study, and covering an element in one post does not cover a demand\nthat it appear in every post. When a quantitative result is required, specify\nthat its value, population or sample, and time/scope are missing.\n\nDo not demand optional breadth or every suggestion in an otherwise complete\nplan. Return JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"plan_item\":10,\"requirement\":\"short concrete requirement\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or only generic\"}]}\n\nList at most four material gaps, ordered by importance. Use a null plan_item\nonly for an explicit user requirement absent from the plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.\n\nPRE-RESEARCH COVERAGE PLAN\n\n1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a seven-section beginner’s guide and executable protocol: interpretation, EHR anatomy/examples, CDC targets, NLP pipeline, prevalence analysis, validation/ethics, and skills/timeline; end with professor questions and next steps.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Assume a novice with basic statistics/programming and no clinical NLP or epidemiology; translate the assignment plainly and progress from raw records through subgroup extraction to defensible disease estimates.\n\n3. CORE: Define EHR, CDC WONDER, structured versus unstructured data, NLP, normalization, race/ethnicity versus ancestry/national origin, autoimmune disease, prevalence, numerator, denominator, confidence interval, validation, precision, recall, and F1.\n\n4. CORE: Show two synthetic Electronic Health Record (EHR) examples—a demographics table and clinical note—marking exact subgroup evidence, then trace one record through extraction, CDC mapping, phenotyping, deduplication, and numeric calculation.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Verify in current CDC WONDER documentation the exact database, years, detailed Asian labels/codebook, coding standards, denominator availability, geography, suppression, export/API limits, and whether it measures prevalence rather than mortality.\n\n6. CORE: Compare dictionaries/regex, clinical named-entity recognition (NER), supervised classifiers, and LLMs for burden, interpretability, privacy, and errors; map structured/textual race and ethnicity evidence across ambiguity, multiracial identity, and missingness without unsupported inference.\n\n7. CORE: Define point prevalence and period prevalence versus incidence, then specify two autoimmune diseases, one EHR phenotype signal each, ICD-10-CM logic, window, numerator, eligible denominator, deduplication, and subgroup confidence intervals.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Find and cite at least one prior measurement jointly examining autoimmune-disease prevalence across detailed Asian subgroups, reporting population, sample size, method, result/performance, and limitations, or explicitly document the evidence gap.\n\n9. CORE: Require an annotation guide, dual manual review, agreement, gold standard, patient-level train/validation/test splits, precision/recall/F1, subgroup error analysis, and missingness/misclassification sensitivity analyses; separate extraction, phenotype, and prevalence validity.\n\n10. SAFETY: Specify institutional governance and secure access despite de-identification, including IRB determination, the applicable HIPAA Privacy Rule pathway, Safe Harbor or Expert Determination, minimum-cell suppression, and prohibition on re-identification.\n\n11. SAFETY: Prohibit treating administrative labels as biological causes or Asian populations as homogeneous; discuss documentation, access, selection, coding, small-cell, and generalizability biases across institutions, regions, periods, and populations.\n\n12. LATENT: Require a data dictionary audit confirming subgroup fields, note types, dates, disease codes, encounter identifiers, and denominator variables remain available after de-identification.\n\n13. CORE: Provide a phased skills plan covering SQL/Python, clinical terminology, NLP, and epidemiology; name one tool each for database querying, annotation, clinical NLP, and prevalence analysis, with professor questions and next steps.\n\n14. BUDGET: Allocate 840 first-draft words: interpretation/definitions 100, EHR example 110, CDC 110, NLP 130, prevalence 140, validation/ethics 130, skills/plan 120; reserve 184 words for evidence-grounded completion patches.\n\nCITED DRAFT TO VERIFY\n\nYour professor probably means: turn free-text clues in de-identified Electronic Health Records, or EHRs, into standardized Asian-subgroup variables matching a specified CDC WONDER classification, then compare the proportion of eligible patients meeting validated autoimmune-disease definitions across those groups. \nAn EHR is a computerized longitudinal record containing structured fields such as demographics, diagnosis codes, medications and laboratory results, plus unstructured narrative notes; NLP converts relevant narrative into analyzable fields. [shard_03549_2575] [shard_03193_44799]\nHere “abstract” means extract and normalize varied expressions to controlled labels, while autoimmune diseases are conditions in which immunity attacks healthy cells, including rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus. [shard_01866_16930]\nTreat race, ethnicity, ancestry and national origin as related but noninterchangeable social variables, retain each label’s wording and source, and never interpret an administrative category as biological ancestry. [shard_03064_71833]\nA synthetic structured record might read “patient_id=P1042; race=Asian; ethnicity=Not Hispanic; detailed_race=Filipino; language=Tagalog,” where “Filipino” is direct subgroup evidence but “Tagalog” alone is not. \nA synthetic note might say, “Patient self-identifies as Filipino American; rheumatology follows her for systemic lupus,” making “Filipino American” and “systemic lupus” the exact spans to extract. \nFor P1042, the pipeline would normalize “Filipino American” to Filipino, combine lupus text with structured diagnostic and clinical evidence, merge duplicate encounters to one patient, and count that patient once in the Filipino lupus numerator. \nBefore NLP, audit whether de-identification preserved detailed demographics, original note types, usable dates, ICD codes, medications, laboratories, patient and encounter identifiers, and variables defining active care. \nCDC WONDER is a collection of separately documented query databases rather than one dataset, so the professor must identify the intended database, release and codebook. [shard_05750_57588]\nIf she means vital-statistics mortality data, the historically prominent death-certificate subgroups are Asian Indian, Chinese, Filipino, Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese, while newer single-race tabulations can include Other Asian and expose as many as 31 race categories. [shard_00350_78120] [shard_03064_71833] [shard_04312_21837]\nThe Multiple Cause of Death database uses ICD-10-coded underlying and contributing causes, covers national through county residence, and supplies denominators for crude and age-adjusted mortality rates, not autoimmune prevalence. [shard_04026_74031] [shard_01519_72088]\nSingle-race results begin in 2018, bridged-race results end in 2020, and those series should not be joined as though their classifications were unchanged. [shard_04312_21837]\nCounts below ten are generally suppressed, detailed county denominators can prevent age-adjusted estimates, and exports should retain query notes because automated access and reproducibility are constrained. [shard_03758_50956] [shard_03567_55288] [shard_00293_53563]\nBegin NLP with an interpretable dictionary of approved labels, spelling variants and abbreviations, plus regular expressions for self-identification, negation, family references and historical mentions. \nClinical named-entity recognition, or NER, marks subgroup mentions and contextual attributes, while normalization maps expressions such as “Filipino-American” to the agreed CDC category. [shard_02454_21127]\nA supervised classifier can exploit broader context but requires manually labeled examples, whereas an LLM may reduce initial engineering yet introduces privacy, hallucination and reproducibility risks and must remain in an approved secure environment. [shard_03468_57413] [shard_02484_70922] [shard_04699_27397]\nPreserve multiracial identities as multiple labels or a prespecified category, distinguish explicit self-report from staff-entered or inferred evidence, and assign unknown when evidence is absent. \nDo not classify from names, language or geography without explicit approval and validation because these proxies overlap and can misclassify people. [shard_03655_15858] [shard_05619_19173]\nPrevalence is the proportion already affected: point prevalence uses cases present on one index date over everyone eligible then, period prevalence uses anyone affected during an interval over that interval’s average eligible population, and incidence instead counts new onsets among people initially disease-free. [shard_04604_9387] [shard_04300_84874]\nA defensible one-year denominator might be unique adults with at least one encounter during 2025 and sufficient prior observation, stratified by extracted subgroup, rather than every historical chart. \nFor lupus, pilot repeated M32 diagnoses or rheumatologist documentation plus supporting antimalarial, ANA, dsDNA or complement evidence; for rheumatoid arthritis, pilot repeated M05/M06 diagnoses plus rheumatology text, disease-modifying medication or rheumatoid-factor evidence. [shard_06487_58913] [shard_06123_32469] [shard_02084_76011]\nIf 12 distinct Filipino patients meet the lupus phenotype among 600 eligible Filipino patients, period prevalence is 12/600=0.020, or 2.0%, reported with a binomial 95% confidence interval and suppressed under the approved small-cell rule when necessary. \nEstimate crude and age/sex-standardized prevalence, never substitute WONDER’s national mortality denominator for the health system’s EHR denominator, and state that results describe the care population because EHR inclusion depends on health-care use. [shard_01425_9658]\nA large English EHR study measured rheumatoid-arthritis incidence and point prevalence, but I could not establish a published autoimmune-prevalence study jointly covering the detailed U.S. Asian categories above. [shard_02084_76011]\nWrite an annotation manual covering allowable evidence, normalization, multiracial cases, uncertainty, negation, relatives and missingness, then have two reviewers independently annotate a stratified sample, calculate agreement, adjudicate disagreements and freeze a gold standard. [shard_06007_6716] [shard_02132_74384]\nSplit by patient, not note, into training, validation and untouched test sets, and report precision, recall and F1 overall and within every subgroup because accuracy can conceal minority-class failures. [shard_02858_24324]\nValidate three separate links: subgroup extraction against human labels, autoimmune phenotypes against specialist chart review, and prevalence against denominator eligibility and deduplication. \nRun sensitivity analyses for missing subgroup data, uncertain mappings, phenotype strictness and plausible misclassification. \nObtain the institution’s IRB or human-subjects determination and privacy approval even for de-identified records, documenting whether HIPAA Safe Harbor or Expert Determination applies, maintaining least-privilege access, suppressing small cells and prohibiting re-identification. [shard_02489_8521] [shard_03621_76660]\nInterpret differences as potentially reflecting documentation, access, selection, coding and institutional geography—not biological causation—and avoid treating Asian populations as homogeneous. [shard_06100_7595]\nUse weeks 1–2 for governance, the data dictionary and a professor-approved WONDER crosswalk; weeks 3–4 for SQL extraction and an annotation pilot; weeks 5–7 for rules or NER development; weeks 8–9 for test-set and chart-review validation; and weeks 10–12 for prevalence estimates, sensitivity analyses and reporting. \nBrush up on SQL, Python, pandas, regular expressions, ICD-10-CM, clinical terminology, NER and classification, confusion-matrix metrics, prevalence denominators, confidence intervals and age standardization. \nA practical tool stack is PostgreSQL for querying, Label Studio for annotation, cTAKES or CLAMP for clinical NLP, and R for prevalence analysis. [shard_01697_66283] [shard_02454_21127]\nAsk the professor which WONDER database, years and subgroup labels she means; whether only explicit self-identification is allowed; which notes and structured fields exist; which diseases, phenotype definitions and observation window she wants; how active patients are defined; and what validation sample, suppression threshold and deliverables are expected.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"plan_item\":8,\"requirement\":\"Give the closest prior measured autoimmune-disease context with population, sample size, method, quantitative result, and limitations, while stating the detailed-Asian-subgroup scope mismatch.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The unnamed English rheumatoid-arthritis study is described only as large and as measuring incidence and prevalence; it lacks sample size, method, quantitative result, limitations, and a concrete comparison with the intended detailed Asian subgroups.\"},{\"plan_item\":1,\"requirement\":\"Present the guide in seven distinct sections covering interpretation, EHR examples, CDC targets, NLP, prevalence, validation/ethics, and skills/timeline.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The content is an unsectioned narrative rather than the explicitly required seven-section deliverable.\"},{\"plan_item\":3,\"requirement\":\"Define numerator, denominator, confidence interval, validation, precision, recall, and F1 for a novice audience.\",\"draft_gap\":\"These terms are used or listed but not plainly defined; in particular, no explanation distinguishes precision, recall, and F1 or explains what a confidence interval and validation mean.\"},{\"plan_item\":7,\"requirement\":\"Specify executable disease-phenotype and denominator logic for both lupus and rheumatoid arthritis, including exact thresholds, logical combinations, observation window, and eligibility rules.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The phenotypes use indeterminate terms such as “repeated,” “plus supporting,” and “sufficient prior observation,” without diagnosis-count thresholds, explicit AND/OR logic, or a defined prior-observation requirement.\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"plan_item": 8, "requirement": "Give the closest prior measured autoimmune-disease context with population, sample size, method, quantitative result, and limitations, while stating the detailed-Asian-subgroup scope mismatch.", "draft_gap": "The unnamed English rheumatoid-arthritis study is describ